test_recon -- recon / 0 and recon / 1 (Nov 2026; parking lot 61)

QUICK START

  sh run_recon_tests.sh [path-to-lagrit]     10 decks, about 10 s
  open summary.html

WHAT IT TESTS

  The aim of recon / 1 (Carl, Nov 4): every Voronoi centre (cell
  circumcentre) inside the exterior boundary, so that two-point flux
  codes (FEHM, PFLOTRAN, TOUGH) get positive coupling coefficients. The
  2D method: M. Murphy and C. Gable, "Strategies for Nonobtuse Boundary
  Delaunay Triangulations", 7th International Meshing Roundtable, 2000.
  3D is harder: M. Murphy, D. Mount and C. Gable, "A point-placement
  strategy for conforming Delaunay tetrahedralization", Int. J. Comput.
  Geom. Appl. 11 (2001) 669-682. The checker counts the circumcentres
  outside the domain (every mesh here is a box: the box of its vertices).

  recon / 0 flips edges (2D) or faces (3D) toward Delaunay and adds no
  vertex. recon / 1 also adds vertices where a boundary triangle has an
  obtuse angle (its Voronoi coefficient is negative):
    2D  reconloop2d: up to 50 rounds of flips (recon2d), then the
        negative coefficients (matbld0tri), then refine_edge_2d puts a
        vertex on each such boundary (or interface) edge, until none is
        left;
    3D  reconloop3d: ONE round of flips (recon2), one pass of
        refine_edge_3d (a vertex at the foot of the altitude on the
        boundary edge opposite each obtuse angle of a boundary face),
        flips again. RECON.md recommends recon / 1 for 2D only.

  rc01_2d_flip       recon / 0, 2D grid with 54 non-Delaunay edges
  rc02_2d_add        recon / 0 then recon / 1 on random points (15 obtuse
                     boundary angles)
  rc03_2d_flip_add   recon / 1 on the rc01 grid
  rc04_2d_materials  two materials, recon / 0 then recon / 1
  rc05_2d_off        ivoronoi = 5 turns recon off
  rc06_2d_poisson    Poisson disk points in the unit square, h = 0.05,
                     connected: recon / 0 then recon / 1 (Carl: a mesh one would
                     compute on, unlike rc02's random points)
  rc07_2d_poisson_graded  the same with h graded 0.01 to 0.05 (the h
                     field of test_poisson_2d)
  rc11_3d_flip       recon / 0, 3D grid with 621 non-Delaunay faces
  rc12_3d_add        recon / 1 on a Delaunay tet mesh (random points)
  rc13_3d_materials  two materials, recon / 0 then recon / 1
  rc14_errors        no cells (WARNING); a hex mesh (message)
  rc15_no_parents    two materials read from AVS, no parent / child
                     vertices

  make_recon.py writes the inputs (fixed seeds) and decks; each deck
  dumps the mesh before and after each recon. check_recon.py judges every
  mesh against the one before it in exact rational arithmetic on the
  dumped coordinates; its opening comment defines every check.

RESULTS (Nov 4 2026)

  PASS   rc01-rc05, rc11, rc14. 2D recon / 0 and recon / 1 do what the
         documentation says: Delaunay; after recon / 1 every Voronoi
         centre inside the domain (rc02: 19 outside before, 0 after, with
         26 vertices added on the boundary) and no obtuse boundary angle;
         the boundary, the area and the area of each material kept.
  rc06, rc07: with its own connect, createpts / poisson_disk / 2d_polygon
         already ends with smooth, recon / 0, smooth, recon / 1
         (poi_driver.f), so these decks make the points with no_connect
         and connect them. Even so, the Poisson disk mesh has no Voronoi
         centre outside the square and no obtuse boundary angle (the
         boundary vertices are placed first, closer together than h),
         and recon / 1 rightly changes nothing. A real-world mesh where
         recon / 1 must add vertices is still wanted.
  rc11   recon / 0 in 3D leaves 145 of 621 faces not Delaunay (it prints
         them as "nface"): its flips (2-3, 3-2, 4-4) cannot reach every
         face. Measured, not failed; the vertices, boundary and volume
         are kept.
  KNOWN  rc12: recon / 1 in 3D does not reach its aim: the Voronoi
         centres outside the domain go from 310 to 454, a Delaunay mesh
         becomes one with 330 faces not Delaunay, and the obtuse boundary
         angles go from 268 to 524 (each new vertex makes new obtuse angles; there is only
         one pass). It writes the file gmv_refine_edge_3d in the current
         folder (a debugging dump left in refine_edge_3d.f). Valid, the
         volume and the boundary surface are kept.
  KNOWN  rc13: recon / 1 in 3D with two materials prints "zero terminated
         parent/child chain" (11 times): new interface vertices are not
         linked into the parent / child chains.
  KNOWN  rc15: a two-material tet mesh read from AVS without parent /
         child vertices: recon / 0 prints "zero terminated parent/child
         chain" and "in fnd2to3i ... not interface point" and LaGriT
         stops in the middle of the deck with exit status 0.

  Also found reading the code (recon.f): the test before contable checks
  the status of an earlier call (icscode, not ics), so contable is never
  called (parking lot 48); the message for a hex mesh says "Refine on
  this CMO type is not implemented".

FILES

  run_recon_tests.sh   runs the decks in work/, keeps any
                       gmv_refine_edge_3d as <deck>.gmv_refine_edge_3d
  make_recon.py        inputs, decks, work/expect.json
  check_recon.py       the checks; logs/<deck>.check, logs/table.txt,
                       summary.html
